// SSH Phase 2 (`docs/ssh-decision.md`): the transport protocol. Version
// exchange, the binary packet protocol (both before and after keys exist),
// `KEXINIT` negotiation, the curve25519-sha256 key exchange, session key
// derivation, and `SSH_MSG_NEWKEYS` -- ending with a `Transport` a future
// Phase 3 (`ssh-userauth`, RFC 4252) and Phase 4 (the connection protocol,
// RFC 4254) build on, touching only `send`/`recv`/`session_id`/`close`.
//
// API
// ---
//   ssh.connect(host, port, expected_host_key) -> Result<Transport, Error>
//   ssh.connect_any(host, port, host_keys)     -> Result<Transport, Error>
//   ssh.presented_host_key(host, port)         -> Result<HostKey, Error>
//   key.fingerprint()  -> str     "SHA256:..." exactly as `ssh-keygen -lf` prints it
//   key.blob_base64()  -> str     the key column of a `known_hosts` line
//   t.send(payload)   -> Result<int, Error>     all of `payload`, or an error
//   t.recv()          -> Result<bytes, Error>   one application message
//   t.session_id()    -> bytes                  for Phase 3's auth signature
//   t.close()         -> Result<bool, Error>
//
// `Link` (below) is the three-method interface Phase 3 (`lib/ssh/sshauth.m31`)
// and Phase 4 (`lib/ssh/sshexec.m31`) are written against -- `Transport`
// satisfies it structurally, and so does the scripted in-memory fake their
// pure tests drive, which is what lets every message parser and state
// machine in those modules be tested with no `sshd` in sight.
//
// The seam, testable with no `sshd` in sight (the same split
// `apps/git/httpfetch.m31` draws, for the same reason -- see its own
// header): every wire-format function below is pure, operating on `bytes`
// already in memory. Only `connect` (via the private `do_handshake`) touches
// a socket.
//
// Per `docs/ssh-decision.md` §2, this client proposes, and accepts, exactly
// one algorithm suite -- curve25519-sha256 / ssh-ed25519 /
// chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com -- and refuses cleanly if a server does not
// offer all three. There is no fallback, ever.
//
// --- `chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com` is not generic AEAD ChaCha20-Poly1305 --
//
// `/usr/share/doc/openssh/PROTOCOL.chacha20poly1305` describes a
// construction built on the *original* (pre-IETF) ChaCha20 word layout, two
// separate 256-bit keys per direction (`K_1`, used only to encrypt the
// 4-byte packet length; `K_2`, used for the Poly1305 one-time key and the
// payload itself), and a nonce that is simply the packet sequence number --
// nothing is exchanged for it. `lib/crypto/chacha20poly1305.m31`'s `seal`/`open`
// implement RFC 8439's *different* construction (a cleartext length, an
// AAD, a single key) and are not used here directly -- only
// `chacha20poly1305.openssh_block` (the matching word layout),
// `chacha20poly1305.mac` (Poly1305 alone) are, plus `consttime.is_equal` for
// the tag check. See Part 6.
//
// --- the shared secret's byte order: the RFC text vs. a real server ---------
//
// RFC 8731 §3.1 reads as if `x25519`'s shared-secret output (little-endian,
// per RFC 7748) must be byte-reversed to big-endian before it becomes the
// exchange hash's `mpint K`. A real `sshd` disagrees: the host-key signature
// only verifies when `x25519`'s raw little-endian output is mpint-encoded
// directly, with NO reversal -- found and confirmed the hard way, with
// `tests/ssh/test_transport.sh`'s live fixture, exactly the kind of gap
// `docs/ssh-decision.md` §4's "the oracle is a real implementation" rule
// exists to catch. Part 4/`do_handshake` does what OpenSSH actually does,
// not what the RFC's own wording suggests.
//
// `mpint` itself (RFC 4251 §5) is not a bare big-endian encoding regardless:
// a leading `0x00` is *required* whenever the leading byte's top bit would
// otherwise be set, true for roughly half of all real shared secrets -- an
// ordinary-handshake case, not an edge case. Handled in `encode_mpint`.
//
// --- what is NOT here (`docs/ssh-decision.md` §1, §3) ------------------------
//
//   - Time-based rekeying (the client rekeys by traffic volume only; a
//     server's rekey, whatever its reason, is answered).
//   - Algorithm fallback of any kind. See above.
//   - Compression (always negotiates "none").
//   - Authentication (`ssh-userauth`, RFC 4252) and the connection protocol
//     (RFC 4254, channels/`exec`) -- Phase 3 and Phase 4, each its own file
//     on top of `Transport` (`lib/ssh/sshauth.m31`, `lib/ssh/sshexec.m31`).
//   - A graceful `SSH_MSG_DISCONNECT` on `close()` -- nothing downstream
//     depends on one yet; a two-line follow-up once something does.
//   - Host-key *management*: `connect`/`connect_any` take the expected
//     key(s) as plain arguments and refuse outright on a mismatch, never
//     trusting on first use; where that expectation comes from is the
//     caller's problem (`lib/ssh/sshhosts.m31` reads and appends to OpenSSH
//     `known_hosts` files). What the library does give a caller who wants to
//     ask a person ("the authenticity of host ... can't be established,
//     fingerprint SHA256:..., continue?") is `presented_host_key`: the key a
//     server offers, proved by its signature, with nothing trusted and no
//     login attempted. The caller shows `key.fingerprint()`, and only if the
//     person agrees records it (`sshhosts.add`) and connects again -- through
//     the same refusing check as any other connection. `Error` stays
//     payload-free (`docs/errors-decision.md` §4), so `HostKeyMismatch`
//     does not carry the key; ask for it with `presented_host_key` instead.

import io;
import net;
import crypto.sha256;
import crypto.chacha20poly1305;
import crypto.consttime;
import crypto.x25519;
import crypto.ed25519;
import crypto.ecdsa;
import crypto.rsa;
import crypto.sha512;
import random;
import timer;
import encoding.base64;
import ssh.sshcipher;

/// Why a handshake, or a later `send`/`recv`, could not be completed.
/// Payload-free (`docs/errors-decision.md` §4): nothing here needs more
/// context than the caller already has.
pub enum Error {
    /// The peer's version line was missing, malformed, or not `SSH-2.x`.
    BadVersion;
    /// A length-prefixed field (a packet, a `string`, a name-list) named
    /// more bytes than were actually present.
    Truncated;
    /// A packet's own length or padding-length field is not self-consistent
    /// (RFC 4253 §6's own bounds), or an encrypted length decoded to
    /// something absurd.
    BadPacketLength;
    /// The computed Poly1305 tag did not match the one a packet carried --
    /// nothing about that packet is trusted past this point.
    MacMismatch;
    /// A message's own type byte was not the one this step of the handshake
    /// (or `recv`) expects.
    UnexpectedMessage;
    /// The server's `KEXINIT` did not offer `curve25519-sha256`.
    NoCommonKex;
    /// The server's `KEXINIT` did not offer `ssh-ed25519`.
    NoCommonHostKey;
    /// The server's `KEXINIT` did not offer `chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com`
    /// in both directions.
    NoCommonCipher;
    /// A host-key or signature blob named an algorithm other than
    /// `ssh-ed25519` -- this client never negotiated anything else, so this
    /// means a confused or malicious server, not a compatibility gap.
    UnsupportedHostKeyType;
    /// The server's host key (whose signature did verify) is none of the keys
    /// the caller expected. `presented_host_key` says which key it was.
    HostKeyMismatch;
    /// The host key's signature over the exchange hash did not verify.
    BadSignature;
    /// The peer sent `SSH_MSG_DISCONNECT`.
    Disconnected;
    /// The underlying connection failed -- a reset, a closed peer. `net`/`io`'s
    /// own error already said which; there is nothing more specific to add
    /// here.
    Io;
    /// The server's key-exchange value or host key was not a valid point or
    /// key (off the curve, a modulus under 2048 bits, ...).
    BadKeyExchange;
    /// A read waited as long as the read timeout allows (`connect_any`'s
    /// `timeout_ms`, `Transport.set_read_timeout`). **The transport cannot be
    /// used afterwards**: the timeout may have fallen part way through a
    /// packet, whose bytes were consumed, so the next packet would be read
    /// from the middle of this one. Close it.
    TimedOut;
}

pub str Error.to_str() {
    match (this) {
        case BadVersion: {
            return "the peer's SSH version line is missing or malformed";
        }
        case Truncated: {
            return "a length-prefixed field named more bytes than arrived";
        }
        case BadPacketLength: {
            return "a packet's length or padding is not self-consistent";
        }
        case MacMismatch: {
            return "a packet's authentication tag did not match";
        }
        case UnexpectedMessage: {
            return "an unexpected message type arrived at this point";
        }
        case NoCommonKex: {
            return "the server offers none of the key exchanges this client accepts (curve25519-sha256, ecdh-sha2-nistp256)";
        }
        case NoCommonHostKey: {
            return "the server offers none of the host key algorithms this client accepts (ssh-ed25519, ecdsa-sha2-nistp256, rsa-sha2-512, rsa-sha2-256)";
        }
        case NoCommonCipher: {
            return "the server offers none of the ciphers this client accepts (chacha20-poly1305, aes-gcm, aes-ctr with hmac-sha2-256)";
        }
        case UnsupportedHostKeyType: {
            return "a host-key or signature blob named an unsupported algorithm";
        }
        case HostKeyMismatch: {
            return "the server's host key does not match the expected one";
        }
        case BadSignature: {
            return "the host key's signature did not verify";
        }
        case Disconnected: {
            return "the peer sent SSH_MSG_DISCONNECT";
        }
        case Io: {
            return "the underlying connection failed";
        }
        case BadKeyExchange: {
            return "the server's key-exchange value or host key is not valid";
        }
        case TimedOut: {
            return "a read timed out waiting for the peer";
        }
    }
}

/// Whether this is `UnsupportedHostKeyType`: a key or signature that is
/// well formed but of a kind (or strength) this client does not use.
pub bool Error.is_unsupported_key() {
    return to_str() == Error.UnsupportedHostKeyType.to_str();
}

// --- message type numbers (RFC 4253) -----------------------------------------

const int SSH_MESSAGE_DISCONNECT = 1;
const int SSH_MESSAGE_IGNORE = 2;
const int SSH_MESSAGE_UNIMPLEMENTED = 3;
const int SSH_MESSAGE_DEBUG = 4;
pub const int SSH_MESSAGE_SERVICE_REQUEST = 5;
pub const int SSH_MESSAGE_SERVICE_ACCEPT = 6;
const int SSH_MESSAGE_KEXINIT = 20;
const int SSH_MESSAGE_NEWKEYS = 21;
const int SSH_MESSAGE_KEX_ECDH_INIT = 30;
const int SSH_MESSAGE_KEX_ECDH_REPLY = 31;
// The one suite this client ever proposes or accepts (`docs/ssh-decision.md`
// §2) -- spelled once, here, rather than repeated at each call site.
const str KEX_ALGO = "curve25519-sha256";
const str HOST_KEY_ALGO = "ssh-ed25519";
const str CIPHER_ALGO = "chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com";

// --- Part 0: wire primitives --------------------------------------------------

// A 32-bit word, big-endian, at `buf[at .. at+4)` -- the caller's own
// responsibility to have already checked those bytes exist, exactly
// `apps/git/httpfetch.m31`'s own `be32`.
int be32(bytes buffer, int offset) {
    return buffer[offset] << 24 | buffer[offset + 1] << 16 | buffer[offset + 2] << 8 | buffer[offset + 3];
}

void push_u32(bytes out, int value) {
    out.push(value >> 24 & 0xFF);
    out.push(value >> 16 & 0xFF);
    out.push(value >> 8 & 0xFF);
    out.push(value & 0xFF);
}

/// One field parsed off the wire: its raw content, and the caller's next
/// offset -- this language has no tuples (`apps/git/FRICTION.md` §2), the
/// same reason `apps/git/httpfetch.m31`'s own `Packet` carries both.
pub type Field {
    pub bytes value;
    pub int next;
}

/// An SSH `string` (RFC 4251 §5): `uint32` length, then that many octets,
/// appended to `out`.
pub void push_string(bytes out, bytes content) {
    push_u32(out, content.size());
    out.extend(content);
}

/// An SSH `string` parsed out of `buf[at ..]`. `Err(Error.Truncated)` if the
/// length field itself, or the content it names, is not all there.
pub Result<Field, Error> parse_string(bytes buffer, int offset) {
    if (offset < 0 || offset + 4 > buffer.size()) {
        return Result<Field, Error>.Err(Error.Truncated);
    }
    int length = be32(buffer, offset);
    if (offset + 4 + length > buffer.size()) {
        return Result<Field, Error>.Err(Error.Truncated);
    }
    bytes value = buffer.substr(offset + 4, offset + 4 + length);
    return Result<Field, Error>.Ok(Field(value, offset + 4 + length));
}

/// A name-list (RFC 4251 §5): an SSH `string` whose content is a
/// comma-separated list of algorithm names, parsed into `List<str>`. Empty
/// content is an empty list, not a one-element list holding `""`.
pub type NameList {
    pub List<str> names;
    pub int next;
}

pub bytes encode_name_list(List<str> names) {
    bytes out = [];
    push_string(out, names.join(",").to_bytes());
    return out;
}

pub Result<NameList, Error> parse_name_list(bytes buffer, int offset) {
    Field field = parse_string(buffer, offset)?;
    List<str> names = [];
    match (field.value.utf8()) {
        case Some(str names_text): {
            if (names_text.size() > 0) {
                for (str part in names_text.split(",")) {
                    names.push(part);
                }
            }
        }
        case None: {
            return Result<NameList, Error>.Err(Error.Truncated);
        }
    }
    return Result<NameList, Error>.Ok(NameList(names, field.next));
}

/// RFC 4251 §5 `mpint`: `be_value`'s big-endian bytes, re-encoded as the
/// shortest unsigned representation (zero is the empty string; a leading
/// `0x00` is added exactly when needed to keep the top bit clear, never
/// otherwise), then wrapped as an SSH `string` -- ready to append into the
/// exchange hash or a packet payload. See this module's header for why the
/// leading-zero rule is not an edge case here.
pub bytes encode_mpint(bytes be_value) {
    bool is_all_zero = true;
    for (int octet in be_value) {
        if (octet != 0) {
            is_all_zero = false;
        }
    }
    bytes out = [];
    if (is_all_zero) {
        push_string(out, []);
        return out;
    }
    int first_nonzero = 0;
    while (be_value[first_nonzero] == 0) {
        first_nonzero = first_nonzero + 1;
    }
    bytes trimmed = be_value.substr(first_nonzero, be_value.size());
    bytes content = [];
    if ((trimmed[0] & 0x80) != 0) {
        content.push(0);
    }
    content.extend(trimmed);
    push_string(out, content);
    return out;
}

// A failed read, as this module's error: the socket's read timeout expiring
// (EAGAIN, 11, which `net` documents as how a timeout arrives through a
// stream method) is `TimedOut`, anything else `Io`. The cases are listed one
// by one because `io.Error`'s `match` has no default.
Error failure_of(io.Error cause) {
    match (cause) {
        case Other(int code): {
            if (code == 11) {
                return Error.TimedOut;
            }
            return Error.Io;
        }
        case NotFound: {
            return Error.Io;
        }
        case PermissionDenied: {
            return Error.Io;
        }
        case IsDirectory: {
            return Error.Io;
        }
        case NotADirectory: {
            return Error.Io;
        }
        case AlreadyExists: {
            return Error.Io;
        }
        case NotEmpty: {
            return Error.Io;
        }
        case InvalidUtf8: {
            return Error.Io;
        }
    }
}

/// Server-supplied text made safe to print: C0 and C1 control characters
/// (except newline and tab), DEL, and the invisible or direction-changing
/// characters (zero-width, bidi overrides and isolates, the BOM) are dropped,
/// so a banner, a name or a message cannot move the cursor, retitle the
/// window, inject escape sequences or reorder what the reader sees. Linear
/// in the length of `text`.
pub str sanitize_text(str text) {
    List<int> kept = [];
    for (int code in text.chars()) {
        if (code == 10 || code == 9) {
            kept.push(code);
        } else if (code < 32 || code == 127 || (code >= 128 && code < 160)) {
        } else if ((code >= 0x200B && code <= 0x200F) || (code >= 0x202A && code <= 0x202E) || (code >= 0x2066 && code <= 0x2069) || code == 0xFEFF) {
        } else {
            kept.push(code);
        }
    }
    return str.from_chars(kept);
}

/// What the transport needs of the byte stream under it. A `net.Conn` is
/// one; `sshjump.ChannelWire` (a TCP connection tunnelled through another
/// SSH session, for `ProxyJump`) is the other. Nothing here is specific to a
/// socket except `fileno`, which an event loop may poll on.
pub interface Wire {
    Result<bytes, io.Error> read(int byte_count);
    Result<bytes, io.Error> read_until(bytes delimiter, int limit);
    Result<int, io.Error> write(bytes data);
    Result<bool, io.Error> close();
    Result<bool, net.Error> set_read_timeout(int timeout_ms);
    Result<bool, net.Error> wait(int timeout_ms);
    int fileno();
}

prim int __poll(List<int> fds, List<int> events, List<int> revents, int timeout_ms);

/// A `net.Conn` as a `Wire` that **never parks on the reactor**: every wait
/// for the socket is a `poll(2)` on the carrier's own thread, so a program
/// with one thread of control (an interactive client) depends on nothing but
/// the kernel for its connection. (A server running many green threads
/// wants the other thing -- the parked wait -- and should use the `net.Conn`
/// itself.) Reads honour the read timeout (`Error.TimedOut`); writes wait for
/// the socket to be writable before each attempt, so `write_some` never
/// finds it full.
pub type PollWire {
    pub net.Conn connection;
    int timeout_ms = 0;
}

// Waits for `events` (1 readable, 4 writable) for up to the read timeout;
// 1 ready, 0 timed out, -1 failed.
int PollWire.await(int events) {
    List<int> fds = [connection.fileno()];
    List<int> wanted = [events];
    int wait_ms = -1;
    if (timeout_ms > 0) {
        wait_ms = timeout_ms;
    }
    while (true) {
        List<int> revents = [];
        int ready = __poll(fds, wanted, revents, wait_ms);
        if (ready == -4) {
            continue;
        }
        if (ready < 0) {
            return -1;
        }
        if (ready == 0) {
            return 0;
        }
        return 1;
    }
}

pub Result<bytes, io.Error> PollWire.read(int byte_count) {
    match (connection.wait(0)) {
        case Ok(bool is_ready): {
            if (!is_ready) {
                int state = await(1);
                if (state == 0) {
                    return Result<bytes, io.Error>.Err(io.Error.Other(11));
                }
                if (state < 0) {
                    return Result<bytes, io.Error>.Err(io.Error.Other(5));
                }
            }
        }
        case Err(_): {
            return Result<bytes, io.Error>.Err(io.Error.Other(5));
        }
    }
    return connection.read(byte_count);
}

pub Result<bytes, io.Error> PollWire.read_until(bytes delimiter, int limit) {
    bytes out = [];
    while (out.size() < limit) {
        bytes one = read(1)?;
        if (one.size() == 0) {
            return Result<bytes, io.Error>.Ok(out);
        }
        out.extend(one);
        if (out.ends_with(delimiter)) {
            return Result<bytes, io.Error>.Ok(out);
        }
    }
    return Result<bytes, io.Error>.Ok(out);
}

pub Result<int, io.Error> PollWire.write(bytes data) {
    int sent = 0;
    while (sent < data.size()) {
        int state = await(4);
        if (state <= 0) {
            return Result<int, io.Error>.Err(io.Error.Other(32));
        }
        int wrote = connection.write_some(data.substr(sent, data.size()))?;
        sent = sent + wrote;
    }
    return Result<int, io.Error>.Ok(sent);
}

pub Result<bool, io.Error> PollWire.close() {
    return connection.close();
}

pub Result<bool, net.Error> PollWire.set_read_timeout(int timeout_milliseconds) {
    if (timeout_milliseconds < 0) {
        trap("PollWire.set_read_timeout: a negative timeout");
    }
    timeout_ms = timeout_milliseconds;
    return Result<bool, net.Error>.Ok(true);
}

pub Result<bool, net.Error> PollWire.wait(int timeout_milliseconds) {
    match (connection.wait(0)) {
        case Ok(bool is_ready): {
            if (is_ready || timeout_milliseconds == 0) {
                return Result<bool, net.Error>.Ok(is_ready);
            }
        }
        case Err(net.Error error): {
            return Result<bool, net.Error>.Err(error);
        }
    }
    List<int> fds = [connection.fileno()];
    List<int> wanted = [1];
    List<int> revents = [];
    int ready = __poll(fds, wanted, revents, timeout_milliseconds);
    if (ready < 0 && ready != -4) {
        return Result<bool, net.Error>.Err(net.Error.Other(5));
    }
    return Result<bool, net.Error>.Ok(ready > 0);
}

pub int PollWire.fileno() {
    return connection.fileno();
}

// Reads exactly `byte_count` bytes from `connection`, looping over `net.Conn.read`'s own
// "1..n bytes, no exact-read primitive" contract. A 0-length read before `byte_count`
// bytes have arrived means the peer is gone.
//
// With `deadline_ns` above 0 (a `timer.monotonic_ns` time) the whole read has
// until then, not each chunk: a peer that sends one byte just inside every
// per-read timeout cannot hold the reader for ever.
Result<bytes, Error> read_exact(Wire connection, int byte_count, int deadline_ns = 0) {
    bytes out = [];
    while (out.size() < byte_count) {
        match (connection.read(byte_count - out.size())) {
            case Ok(bytes chunk): {
                if (chunk.size() == 0) {
                    return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.Io);
                }
                out.extend(chunk);
                if (deadline_ns > 0 && out.size() < byte_count && timer.monotonic_ns() > deadline_ns) {
                    return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.TimedOut);
                }
            }
            case Err(io.Error cause): {
                return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(failure_of(cause));
            }
        }
    }
    return Result<bytes, Error>.Ok(out);
}

// --- Part 1: version exchange (RFC 4253 §4.2) --------------------------------

const str CLIENT_VERSION_STR = "SSH-2.0-m31_0.1";

// Both version strings, verbatim minus their trailing CRLF -- fed unmodified
// into the exchange hash later (Part 4). Private: version exchange is one of
// the two functions in this module that touches a socket, tested only
// against a real `sshd`, per this module's own header.
type VersionPair {
    bytes v_c;
    bytes v_s;
}

Result<VersionPair, Error> exchange_version(Wire connection) {
    bytes v_c = CLIENT_VERSION_STR.to_bytes();
    bytes line = clone(v_c);
    line.push(13);
    line.push(10);
    match (connection.write(line)) {
        case Ok(_): {
        }
        case Err(_): {
            return Result<VersionPair, Error>.Err(Error.Io);
        }
    }
    bytes crlf = [13, 10];
    bytes ssh_prefix = "SSH-".to_bytes();
    bytes ssh2_prefix = "SSH-2.".to_bytes();
    int tries = 0;
    while (true) {
        if (tries >= 20) {
            return Result<VersionPair, Error>.Err(Error.BadVersion);
        }
        tries = tries + 1;
        bytes raw = [];
        match (connection.read_until(crlf, 255)) {
            case Ok(bytes received): {
                raw = received;
            }
            case Err(io.Error cause): {
                return Result<VersionPair, Error>.Err(failure_of(cause));
            }
        }
        if (!raw.ends_with(crlf)) {
            return Result<VersionPair, Error>.Err(Error.BadVersion);
        }
        bytes v_s = raw.substr(0, raw.size() - 2);
        if (v_s.starts_with(ssh_prefix)) {
            if (!v_s.starts_with(ssh2_prefix)) {
                return Result<VersionPair, Error>.Err(Error.BadVersion);
            }
            return Result<VersionPair, Error>.Ok(VersionPair(v_c, v_s));
        }
        // RFC 4253 §4.2: the server MAY send other lines before its version
        // line, which a client MUST tolerate -- loop and read another.
    }
}

// --- Part 2: binary packet protocol, plaintext framing (pre-keys) -----------

const int MIN_PADDING = 4;
const int BLOCK_SIZE = 8;

// RFC 4253 §6's padding rule, for the PLAINTEXT framing only (Part 2): the
// smallest value in `[MIN_PADDING, 255]` making the WHOLE wire packet -- the
// 4-byte `packet_length` field itself, `padding_length`, `payload` and the
// random padding, concatenated -- a multiple of `BLOCK_SIZE`. The RFC's own
// wording is explicit that the length field is part of what gets aligned,
// not just the value it names: "the length of the concatenation of
// 'packet_length', 'padding_length', 'payload', and 'random padding' MUST be
// a multiple of the cipher block size or 8" -- so this is `4 + 1 +
// payload_len + pad`, not `1 + payload_len + pad`. Confirmed against a real
// `sshd` (`tests/ssh/test_transport.sh`): without the `+4` it rejected this
// client's own `KEXINIT` with "padding error ... block 8 mod 4" before any
// cipher existed to blame.
//
// **`chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com`'s ciphered framing (Part 6) does NOT
// use this function -- see `compute_padding_len_ciphered` below, and do not
// "simplify" these back into one.** The two genuinely differ: this cipher
// encrypts its 4-byte length field with a wholly separate keystream (`K_1`),
// so that field is never part of the block-aligned run the way it is for an
// ordinary block cipher RFC 4253 §6 was written for. Confirmed the same way,
// against the same real `sshd`: using *this* function for Part 6 produced
// "padding error ... block 8 mod 4" on the very first post-`NEWKEYS` packet,
// even though the plaintext handshake before it (using this exact rule) had
// just succeeded -- the cipher's own padding target is smaller.
int compute_padding_length(int payload_length) {
    int pad = BLOCK_SIZE - (5 + payload_length) % BLOCK_SIZE;
    if (pad < MIN_PADDING) {
        pad = pad + BLOCK_SIZE;
    }
    return pad;
}

// The padding rule for `chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com`'s own framing (Part
// 6): `padding_length + payload + random padding` alone -- never the 4-byte
// length field, which this cipher always encrypts separately. See
// `compute_padding_len`'s own comment for why these two are not the same
// function, confirmed against a real `sshd`.
int compute_padding_length_ciphered(int payload_length) {
    int pad = BLOCK_SIZE - (1 + payload_length) % BLOCK_SIZE;
    if (pad < MIN_PADDING) {
        pad = pad + BLOCK_SIZE;
    }
    return pad;
}

/// One packet's payload, and the caller's next offset -- used for both the
/// plaintext framing below and, once a whole ciphered packet has been
/// decrypted, to strip its own padding (Part 6).
pub type RawPacket {
    pub bytes payload;
    pub int next;
}

/// RFC 4253 §6, before any cipher exists: `uint32 packet_length`, `byte
/// padding_length`, `payload`, random padding -- no MAC.
pub bytes encode_plain_packet(bytes payload, random.System rng) {
    int pad_length = compute_padding_length(payload.size());
    int packet_length = 1 + payload.size() + pad_length;
    bytes out = [];
    push_u32(out, packet_length);
    out.push(pad_length);
    out.extend(payload);
    out.extend(rng.octets(pad_length));
    return out;
}

pub Result<RawPacket, Error> parse_plain_packet(bytes buffer, int offset) {
    if (offset < 0 || offset + 4 > buffer.size()) {
        return Result<RawPacket, Error>.Err(Error.Truncated);
    }
    int packet_length = be32(buffer, offset);
    if (packet_length < 1 || offset + 4 + packet_length > buffer.size()) {
        return Result<RawPacket, Error>.Err(Error.Truncated);
    }
    int pad_length = buffer[offset + 4];
    if (pad_length < MIN_PADDING || pad_length > packet_length - 1) {
        return Result<RawPacket, Error>.Err(Error.BadPacketLength);
    }
    int payload_end = offset + 4 + packet_length - pad_length;
    bytes payload = buffer.substr(offset + 5, payload_end);
    return Result<RawPacket, Error>.Ok(RawPacket(payload, offset + 4 + packet_length));
}

// Reads one whole plaintext packet off `connection` and hands back just its
// payload -- the length is sent in clear at this stage, so the total size
// is known as soon as the first four bytes arrive, and this reuses
// `parse_plain_packet` on the assembled buffer rather than duplicating its
// padding-bounds logic.
Result<bytes, Error> recv_plain_payload(Wire connection) {
    bytes length_bytes = read_exact(connection, 4)?;
    int packet_length = be32(length_bytes, 0);
    if (packet_length < 1 || packet_length > 35000) {
        return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.BadPacketLength);
    }
    bytes rest = read_exact(connection, packet_length)?;
    bytes whole = clone(length_bytes);
    whole.extend(rest);
    RawPacket raw_packet = parse_plain_packet(whole, 0)?;
    return Result<bytes, Error>.Ok(raw_packet.payload);
}

Result<void, Error> send_plain_payload(Wire connection, bytes payload, random.System rng) {
    bytes pkt = encode_plain_packet(payload, rng);
    match (connection.write(pkt)) {
        case Ok(_): {
            return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
        }
        case Err(_): {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.Io);
        }
    }
}

// --- Part 3: KEXINIT and algorithm negotiation (RFC 4253 §7.1) ---------------

/// One `KEXINIT` message, parsed or about to be built. `raw` is the exact
/// payload bytes this was parsed from (or, for this client's own, that
/// `build_kexinit` produced) -- needed verbatim, unmodified, as `I_C`
/// or `I_S` in the exchange hash (Part 4).
pub type KexInit {
    pub bytes cookie;
    pub List<str> kex_algorithms;
    pub List<str> server_host_key_algorithms;
    pub List<str> encryption_algorithms_client_to_server;
    pub List<str> encryption_algorithms_server_to_client;
    pub List<str> mac_algorithms_client_to_server;
    pub List<str> mac_algorithms_server_to_client;
    pub List<str> compression_algorithms_client_to_server;
    pub List<str> compression_algorithms_server_to_client;
    pub List<str> languages_client_to_server;
    pub List<str> languages_server_to_client;
    pub bool is_first_kex_packet_following;
    pub bytes raw;
}

/// What this client is willing to speak, each list in order of preference
/// (RFC 4253 §7.1: the algorithm used is the first one on the *client's*
/// list that the server also lists). **Nothing weaker than what is listed
/// here is ever used**: there is no `none` cipher, no SHA-1, no CBC, no
/// `diffie-hellman-group1`, no `ssh-rsa` signatures over SHA-1, no
/// compression. `kex-strict-c-v00@openssh.com` is a marker, not a key
/// exchange: it asks a server that understands it to apply "strict kex"
/// (see `exchange_keys`).
pub type Offer {
    pub List<str> kex;
    pub List<str> host_keys;
    pub List<str> ciphers;
    pub List<str> macs;
}

const str KEX_ECDH_P256 = "ecdh-sha2-nistp256";
const str KEX_C25519_ALIAS = "curve25519-sha256@libssh.org";
const str KEX_STRICT_CLIENT = "kex-strict-c-v00@openssh.com";
const str KEX_STRICT_SERVER = "kex-strict-s-v00@openssh.com";
const str HOST_KEY_ECDSA_P256 = "ecdsa-sha2-nistp256";
const str HOST_KEY_RSA = "ssh-rsa";
const str SIG_RSA_SHA512 = "rsa-sha2-512";
const str SIG_RSA_SHA256 = "rsa-sha2-256";
const str CIPHER_GCM256 = "aes256-gcm@openssh.com";
const str CIPHER_GCM128 = "aes128-gcm@openssh.com";
const str CIPHER_CTR256 = "aes256-ctr";
const str CIPHER_CTR128 = "aes128-ctr";
const str MAC_ETM = "hmac-sha2-256-etm@openssh.com";
const str MAC_PLAIN = "hmac-sha2-256";

/// The full offer, strongest first.
pub Offer default_offer() {
    return Offer([KEX_ALGO, KEX_C25519_ALIAS, KEX_ECDH_P256, KEX_STRICT_CLIENT], [HOST_KEY_ALGO, HOST_KEY_ECDSA_P256, SIG_RSA_SHA512, SIG_RSA_SHA256], [CIPHER_ALGO, CIPHER_GCM256, CIPHER_GCM128, CIPHER_CTR256, CIPHER_CTR128], [MAC_ETM, MAC_PLAIN]);
}

/// The default offer with `host_key_algorithms` first, in that order, then
/// the rest of the defaults not already named: how a client that holds a
/// `known_hosts` entry of one type asks the server for that type (a server
/// presents the first of the client's list it has; OpenSSH does the same).
/// Names that are not host-key algorithms this client implements are
/// ignored, so a file's `ssh-dss` line cannot widen the offer.
pub Offer offer_preferring(List<str> host_key_algorithms) {
    Offer offer = default_offer();
    List<str> ordered = [];
    for (str given in host_key_algorithms) {
        // A key *type* from a known_hosts line; RSA's signature algorithms
        // are the SHA-2 ones (never `ssh-rsa`, which is SHA-1).
        List<str> names = [given];
        if (given == HOST_KEY_RSA) {
            names = [SIG_RSA_SHA512, SIG_RSA_SHA256];
        }
        for (str name in names) {
            if (offer.host_keys.contains(name) && !ordered.contains(name)) {
                ordered.push(name);
            }
        }
    }
    for (str name in offer.host_keys) {
        if (!ordered.contains(name)) {
            ordered.push(name);
        }
    }
    offer.host_keys = ordered;
    return offer;
}

/// This client's `KEXINIT` for `offer`: empty MAC lists when no CTR cipher
/// is offered (the AEAD ciphers authenticate themselves), `"none"`
/// compression, no languages.
pub bytes build_kexinit(random.System rng, Offer offer) {
    List<str> empty = [];
    bytes out = [];
    out.push(SSH_MESSAGE_KEXINIT);
    out.extend(rng.octets(16));
    out.extend(encode_name_list(offer.kex));
    out.extend(encode_name_list(offer.host_keys));
    out.extend(encode_name_list(offer.ciphers));
    out.extend(encode_name_list(offer.ciphers));
    out.extend(encode_name_list(offer.macs));
    out.extend(encode_name_list(offer.macs));
    out.extend(encode_name_list(["none"]));
    out.extend(encode_name_list(["none"]));
    out.extend(encode_name_list(empty));
    out.extend(encode_name_list(empty));
    out.push(0);
    push_u32(out, 0);
    return out;
}

/// `build_kexinit` with `default_offer()`.
pub bytes build_client_kexinit(random.System rng) {
    return build_kexinit(rng, default_offer());
}

pub Result<KexInit, Error> parse_kexinit(bytes payload) {
    if (payload.size() < 1 || payload[0] != SSH_MESSAGE_KEXINIT) {
        return Result<KexInit, Error>.Err(Error.UnexpectedMessage);
    }
    if (payload.size() < 17) {
        return Result<KexInit, Error>.Err(Error.Truncated);
    }
    bytes cookie = payload.substr(1, 17);
    NameList kex = parse_name_list(payload, 17)?;
    NameList host_key = parse_name_list(payload, kex.next)?;
    NameList enc_c2s = parse_name_list(payload, host_key.next)?;
    NameList enc_s2c = parse_name_list(payload, enc_c2s.next)?;
    NameList mac_c2s = parse_name_list(payload, enc_s2c.next)?;
    NameList mac_s2c = parse_name_list(payload, mac_c2s.next)?;
    NameList comp_c2s = parse_name_list(payload, mac_s2c.next)?;
    NameList comp_s2c = parse_name_list(payload, comp_c2s.next)?;
    NameList lang_c2s = parse_name_list(payload, comp_s2c.next)?;
    NameList lang_s2c = parse_name_list(payload, lang_c2s.next)?;
    int flags_offset = lang_s2c.next;
    if (flags_offset + 1 + 4 > payload.size()) {
        return Result<KexInit, Error>.Err(Error.Truncated);
    }
    bool has_following_packet = payload[flags_offset] != 0;
    return Result<KexInit, Error>.Ok(KexInit(cookie, kex.names, host_key.names, enc_c2s.names, enc_s2c.names, mac_c2s.names, mac_s2c.names, comp_c2s.names, comp_s2c.names, lang_c2s.names, lang_s2c.names, has_following_packet, payload));
}

/// What was agreed.
pub type Suite {
    pub str kex;
    pub str host_key;
    pub str cipher_client_to_server;
    pub str cipher_server_to_client;
    pub str mac_client_to_server;
    pub str mac_server_to_client;
    /// The server also speaks strict kex (RFC-less, OpenSSH 9.6's Terrapin
    /// fix): sequence numbers restart at 0 after NEWKEYS.
    pub bool is_strict;
}

// The first of `ours` that `theirs` also lists, or "".
str first_common(List<str> ours, List<str> theirs, List<str> excluded) {
    for (str name in ours) {
        if (theirs.contains(name) && !excluded.contains(name)) {
            return name;
        }
    }
    return "";
}

bool is_aead(str cipher) {
    return cipher == CIPHER_ALGO || cipher == CIPHER_GCM256 || cipher == CIPHER_GCM128;
}

/// RFC 4253 §7.1 negotiation of `offer` against the server's `KEXINIT`.
/// The three refusals are separate and named; a partial agreement is never
/// returned. `kex-strict-*` markers are never chosen as an algorithm.
pub Result<Suite, Error> negotiate(Offer offer, KexInit server) {
    List<str> markers = [KEX_STRICT_CLIENT, KEX_STRICT_SERVER];
    str kex = first_common(offer.kex, server.kex_algorithms, markers);
    if (kex == "") {
        return Result<Suite, Error>.Err(Error.NoCommonKex);
    }
    List<str> none = [];
    str host_key = first_common(offer.host_keys, server.server_host_key_algorithms, none);
    if (host_key == "") {
        return Result<Suite, Error>.Err(Error.NoCommonHostKey);
    }
    str cipher_c2s = first_common(offer.ciphers, server.encryption_algorithms_client_to_server, none);
    str cipher_s2c = first_common(offer.ciphers, server.encryption_algorithms_server_to_client, none);
    if (cipher_c2s == "" || cipher_s2c == "") {
        return Result<Suite, Error>.Err(Error.NoCommonCipher);
    }
    str mac_c2s = "";
    str mac_s2c = "";
    if (!is_aead(cipher_c2s)) {
        mac_c2s = first_common(offer.macs, server.mac_algorithms_client_to_server, none);
        if (mac_c2s == "") {
            return Result<Suite, Error>.Err(Error.NoCommonCipher);
        }
    }
    if (!is_aead(cipher_s2c)) {
        mac_s2c = first_common(offer.macs, server.mac_algorithms_server_to_client, none);
        if (mac_s2c == "") {
            return Result<Suite, Error>.Err(Error.NoCommonCipher);
        }
    }
    bool is_strict = offer.kex.contains(KEX_STRICT_CLIENT) && server.kex_algorithms.contains(KEX_STRICT_SERVER);
    return Result<Suite, Error>.Ok(Suite(kex, host_key, cipher_c2s, cipher_s2c, mac_c2s, mac_s2c, is_strict));
}

/// `negotiate` against `default_offer()`, as a yes/no: `Ok` means a
/// connection with this server can proceed.
pub Result<void, Error> check_server_kexinit(KexInit server) {
    negotiate(default_offer(), server)?;
    return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
}

// --- Part 4: key exchange (RFC 8731 / RFC 5656 §4 / RFC 8709) ----------------

pub bytes build_kex_ecdh_init(bytes q_c) {
    bytes out = [];
    out.push(SSH_MESSAGE_KEX_ECDH_INIT);
    push_string(out, q_c);
    return out;
}

// `K_S` (host key blob), `Q_S` (server's ephemeral public key) and the
// signature blob, exactly as `SSH_MSG_KEX_ECDH_REPLY` carries them.
type KexEcdhReply {
    bytes k_s;
    bytes q_s;
    bytes signature;
}

Result<KexEcdhReply, Error> parse_kex_ecdh_reply(bytes payload) {
    if (payload.size() < 1 || payload[0] != SSH_MESSAGE_KEX_ECDH_REPLY) {
        return Result<KexEcdhReply, Error>.Err(Error.UnexpectedMessage);
    }
    Field k_s = parse_string(payload, 1)?;
    Field q_s = parse_string(payload, k_s.next)?;
    Field sig = parse_string(payload, q_s.next)?;
    if (sig.next != payload.size()) {
        return Result<KexEcdhReply, Error>.Err(Error.BadKeyExchange);
    }
    return Result<KexEcdhReply, Error>.Ok(KexEcdhReply(k_s.value, q_s.value, sig.value));
}

// `K_S` and a signature blob share the same shape (RFC 8709): `string
// "ssh-ed25519"` then `string <raw octets>`. A name other than
// `ssh-ed25519` means a confused or malicious server -- this client never
// negotiated anything else.
Result<bytes, Error> parse_blob(bytes blob) {
    Field name = parse_string(blob, 0)?;
    match (name.value.utf8()) {
        case Some(str algorithm_name): {
            if (algorithm_name != HOST_KEY_ALGO) {
                return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedHostKeyType);
            }
        }
        case None: {
            return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedHostKeyType);
        }
    }
    Field payload = parse_string(blob, name.next)?;
    return Result<bytes, Error>.Ok(payload.value);
}

/// The raw 32-octet `ssh-ed25519` key inside a `K_S` blob.
pub Result<bytes, Error> parse_host_key_blob(bytes blob) {
    return parse_blob(blob);
}

/// The raw 64-octet `ssh-ed25519` signature inside a signature blob.
pub Result<bytes, Error> parse_signature_blob(bytes blob) {
    return parse_blob(blob);
}

/// `H`, RFC 5656 §4: `HASH(string V_C || string V_S || string I_C ||
/// string I_S || string K_S || string Q_C || string Q_S || mpint K)`, with
/// SHA-256 (what both `curve25519-sha256` and `ecdh-sha2-nistp256` use).
/// `k_mpint` is already the full, length-prefixed encoding `encode_mpint`
/// produces, so it is appended verbatim rather than wrapped again.
pub bytes exchange_hash(bytes v_c, bytes v_s, bytes i_c, bytes i_s, bytes k_s, bytes q_c, bytes q_s, bytes k_mpint) {
    bytes hash_input = [];
    push_string(hash_input, v_c);
    push_string(hash_input, v_s);
    push_string(hash_input, i_c);
    push_string(hash_input, i_s);
    push_string(hash_input, k_s);
    push_string(hash_input, q_c);
    push_string(hash_input, q_s);
    hash_input.extend(k_mpint);
    return sha256.sum(hash_input);
}

// --- host keys of every type this client accepts -------------------------------

// The octets of an SSH `mpint` content (its leading zero removed), as a
// fixed-width big-endian value; `None` if it does not fit or is negative.
Option<bytes> mpint_fixed(bytes content, int width) {
    if (content.size() == 0) {
        return Option<bytes>.None;
    }
    if ((content[0] & 0x80) != 0) {
        return Option<bytes>.None;
    }
    int start = 0;
    while (start < content.size() && content[start] == 0) {
        start = start + 1;
    }
    int significant = content.size() - start;
    if (significant > width) {
        return Option<bytes>.None;
    }
    bytes out = [0; width];
    int index = 0;
    while (index < significant) {
        out[width - significant + index] = content[start + index];
        index = index + 1;
    }
    return Option<bytes>.Some(out);
}

/// Parses a host-key blob of any accepted type into a `HostKey`, checking
/// that its shape is valid for the type: ed25519 (32 octets), ecdsa-sha2-
/// nistp256 (curve name `nistp256` and a 65-octet uncompressed point that is
/// on the curve), ssh-rsa (an odd modulus of 2048 to 4096 bits and a usable
/// exponent). A type this client does not implement, or an RSA key outside
/// 2048-4096 bits, is `UnsupportedHostKeyType` (well formed, but not usable);
/// a malformed key of a known type is `BadKeyExchange`.
pub Result<HostKey, Error> parse_host_key(bytes blob) {
    Field name = parse_string(blob, 0)?;
    str algorithm = "";
    match (name.value.utf8()) {
        case Some(str text): {
            algorithm = text;
        }
        case None: {
            return Result<HostKey, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedHostKeyType);
        }
    }
    if (algorithm == HOST_KEY_ALGO) {
        Field key = parse_string(blob, name.next)?;
        if (key.value.size() != 32 || key.next != blob.size()) {
            return Result<HostKey, Error>.Err(Error.BadKeyExchange);
        }
        return Result<HostKey, Error>.Ok(HostKey(algorithm, blob, key.value));
    }
    if (algorithm == HOST_KEY_ECDSA_P256) {
        Field curve = parse_string(blob, name.next)?;
        Field point = parse_string(blob, curve.next)?;
        if (curve.value != "nistp256".to_bytes() || point.next != blob.size()) {
            return Result<HostKey, Error>.Err(Error.BadKeyExchange);
        }
        match (ecdsa.parse_public_key(ecdsa.p256(), point.value)) {
            case Some(_): {
                return Result<HostKey, Error>.Ok(HostKey(algorithm, blob, point.value));
            }
            case None: {
                return Result<HostKey, Error>.Err(Error.BadKeyExchange);
            }
        }
    }
    if (algorithm == HOST_KEY_RSA) {
        Field exponent = parse_string(blob, name.next)?;
        Field modulus = parse_string(blob, exponent.next)?;
        if (modulus.next != blob.size()) {
            return Result<HostKey, Error>.Err(Error.BadKeyExchange);
        }
        match (rsa.parse_public_key(modulus.value, exponent.value)) {
            case Some(_): {
                bytes none = [];
                return Result<HostKey, Error>.Ok(HostKey(algorithm, blob, none));
            }
            case None: {
                return Result<HostKey, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedHostKeyType);
            }
        }
    }
    return Result<HostKey, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedHostKeyType);
}

// Verifies the server's signature `signature` (a `string alg, string data`
// blob) over `message` with `key`; `algorithm` is the host-key algorithm
// that was negotiated, which the signature's own name must equal (for RSA
// the key type is `ssh-rsa` and the signature name `rsa-sha2-256/512`).
Result<void, Error> verify_host_signature(HostKey key, str algorithm, bytes message, bytes signature) {
    Field name = parse_string(signature, 0)?;
    Field data = parse_string(signature, name.next)?;
    if (data.next != signature.size()) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.Truncated);
    }
    match (name.value.utf8()) {
        case Some(str text): {
            if (text != algorithm) {
                return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedHostKeyType);
            }
        }
        case None: {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedHostKeyType);
        }
    }
    if (key.algorithm == HOST_KEY_ALGO) {
        if (algorithm != HOST_KEY_ALGO) {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedHostKeyType);
        }
        if (data.value.size() != 64 || !ed25519.is_valid_signature(key.key, message, data.value)) {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.BadSignature);
        }
        return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
    }
    if (key.algorithm == HOST_KEY_ECDSA_P256) {
        if (algorithm != HOST_KEY_ECDSA_P256) {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedHostKeyType);
        }
        Field r_field = parse_string(data.value, 0)?;
        Field s_field = parse_string(data.value, r_field.next)?;
        if (s_field.next != data.value.size()) {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.BadSignature);
        }
        bytes raw = [];
        match (mpint_fixed(r_field.value, 32)) {
            case Some(bytes r_value): {
                raw.extend(r_value);
            }
            case None: {
                return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.BadSignature);
            }
        }
        match (mpint_fixed(s_field.value, 32)) {
            case Some(bytes s_value): {
                raw.extend(s_value);
            }
            case None: {
                return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.BadSignature);
            }
        }
        match (ecdsa.parse_public_key(ecdsa.p256(), key.key)) {
            case Some(ecdsa.PublicKey point): {
                if (!ecdsa.is_valid_raw_signature(ecdsa.p256(), point, sha256.sum(message), raw)) {
                    return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.BadSignature);
                }
                return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
            }
            case None: {
                return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.BadKeyExchange);
            }
        }
    }
    if (key.algorithm == HOST_KEY_RSA) {
        rsa.Hash hash = rsa.Hash.Sha256;
        bytes digest = [];
        if (algorithm == SIG_RSA_SHA256) {
            digest = sha256.sum(message);
        } else if (algorithm == SIG_RSA_SHA512) {
            hash = rsa.Hash.Sha512;
            digest = sha512.sum(message);
        } else {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedHostKeyType);
        }
        Field exponent = parse_string(key.blob, 0)?;
        Field e_field = parse_string(key.blob, exponent.next)?;
        Field n_field = parse_string(key.blob, e_field.next)?;
        match (rsa.parse_public_key(n_field.value, e_field.value)) {
            case Some(rsa.PublicKey public_key): {
                if (!rsa.is_valid_pkcs1_v15_signature(public_key, hash, digest, data.value)) {
                    return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.BadSignature);
                }
                return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
            }
            case None: {
                return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedHostKeyType);
            }
        }
    }
    return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedHostKeyType);
}

// --- Part 5: key derivation (RFC 4253 §7.2) -------------------------------------

/// `size` octets of key material for `letter` (A..F): `K1 = HASH(K || H ||
/// letter || session_id)`, `K(n+1) = HASH(K || H || K1 || ... || Kn)`, as
/// many blocks as `size` needs, truncated. `HASH` is SHA-256 here.
pub bytes derive(bytes k_mpint, bytes exchange_digest, int letter, bytes session_id, int size) {
    bytes buf1 = clone(k_mpint);
    buf1.extend(exchange_digest);
    buf1.push(letter);
    buf1.extend(session_id);
    bytes material = sha256.sum(buf1);
    while (material.size() < size) {
        bytes next_input = clone(k_mpint);
        next_input.extend(exchange_digest);
        next_input.extend(material);
        material.extend(sha256.sum(next_input));
    }
    return material.substr(0, size);
}

/// 64 bytes of key material for one direction's `letter`: `derive` with
/// `size` 64, what `chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com` takes per direction.
pub bytes derive64(bytes k_mpint, bytes exchange_digest, int letter, bytes session_id) {
    return derive(k_mpint, exchange_digest, letter, session_id, 64);
}

// --- Part 6: the packet ciphers (see lib/ssh/sshcipher.m31) ----------------------

/// One ciphered packet of `chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com`, kept for its
/// tests: `PROTOCOL.chacha20poly1305`'s construction exactly. `seq` is this
/// direction's running sequence number.
pub bytes encrypt_packet(bytes payload, bytes length_key, bytes payload_key, int seq, random.System rng) {
    bytes material = clone(payload_key);
    material.extend(length_key);
    return sshcipher.new_chacha(material).seal(payload, seq, rng);
}

/// The reverse of `encrypt_packet`, given the three wire components already
/// read -- **verifies the tag before decrypting anything**.
pub Result<bytes, Error> decrypt_packet(bytes length_ct, bytes rest_ct, bytes tag, bytes length_key, bytes payload_key, int seq) {
    bytes material = clone(payload_key);
    material.extend(length_key);
    bytes rest = clone(rest_ct);
    rest.extend(tag);
    return cipher_result(sshcipher.new_chacha(material).open(length_ct, rest, seq));
}

Result<bytes, Error> cipher_result(Result<bytes, sshcipher.Error> result) {
    match (result) {
        case Ok(bytes payload): {
            return Result<bytes, Error>.Ok(payload);
        }
        case Err(sshcipher.Error error): {
            match (error) {
                case BadPacketLength: {
                    return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.BadPacketLength);
                }
                case MacMismatch: {
                    return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.MacMismatch);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

// One packet off the wire through `cipher`: the header, the length check,
// the rest, then open (which authenticates before it trusts anything).
Result<bytes, Error> recv_ciphered_payload(Wire connection, sshcipher.Cipher cipher, int seq, int budget_ms = 0) {
    bytes header = read_exact(connection, cipher.header_size())?;
    int deadline_ns = 0;
    if (budget_ms > 0) {
        deadline_ns = timer.monotonic_ns() + budget_ms * 1000000;
    }
    int remaining = 0;
    match (cipher.remaining(header, seq)) {
        case Ok(int count): {
            remaining = count;
        }
        case Err(_): {
            return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.BadPacketLength);
        }
    }
    bytes rest = read_exact(connection, remaining, deadline_ns: deadline_ns)?;
    return cipher_result(cipher.open(header, rest, seq));
}

Result<void, Error> send_ciphered_payload(Wire connection, bytes payload, sshcipher.Cipher cipher, int seq, random.System rng) {
    bytes pkt = cipher.seal(payload, seq, rng);
    match (connection.write(pkt)) {
        case Ok(_): {
            return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
        }
        case Err(_): {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.Io);
        }
    }
}

// --- Part 7: SSH_MSG_NEWKEYS and the Transport type --------------------------

/// What the layers above the transport need from one, and nothing more:
/// `lib/ssh/sshauth.m31` and `lib/ssh/sshexec.m31` take a `Link`, never a
/// `Transport`, so a test can hand them a scripted in-memory peer.
pub interface Link {
    Result<int, Error> send(bytes payload);
    Result<bytes, Error> recv();
    bytes session_id();
}

/// A live, key-exchanged SSH connection: `send`/`recv` carry application
/// payloads, already encrypted/authenticated in both directions. Every
/// field is private -- the only way to build one is `ssh.connect`.
pub type Transport {
    Wire connection;
    int send_seq;
    int recv_seq;
    sshcipher.Cipher send_cipher;
    sshcipher.Cipher recv_cipher;
    bytes sid;
    random.System rng;
    Suite negotiated;
    // What a rekey (server-initiated, RFC 4253 §9) needs again: the version
    // strings and the offer, and the host key's blob, which must not change.
    VersionPair versions;
    Offer offer;
    bytes host_key_blob;
    // Payload bytes sent and received since the last key exchange, the
    // limit at which this client starts the next one (0: never), the
    // application packets that arrived while a rekey it started was waiting
    // for the server's KEXINIT, and whether one is under way.
    int traffic;
    int rekey_limit;
    List<bytes> queued;
    bool is_rekeying;
    // The read timeout also bounds the body of one packet in total.
    int read_budget_ms;
}

/// This connection's first exchange hash `H`, the session identifier for
/// its whole life (a rekey does not change it), needed verbatim by the
/// publickey-auth signature (RFC 4252 §7).
pub bytes Transport.session_id() {
    return sid;
}

/// What was negotiated, for a caller that wants to show it.
pub Suite Transport.suite() {
    return negotiated;
}

/// Encrypts, pads and authenticates `payload` as one packet and writes it.
/// Mirrors `net.Conn.write`'s own "all of it, or an error" contract.
pub Result<int, Error> Transport.send(bytes payload) {
    if (!is_rekeying && rekey_limit > 0 && traffic >= rekey_limit) {
        start_rekey()?;
    }
    send_ciphered_payload(connection, payload, send_cipher, send_seq, rng)?;
    send_seq = send_seq + 1;
    traffic = traffic + payload.size();
    return Result<int, Error>.Ok(payload.size());
}

/// Changes how much traffic passes before this client starts a rekey
/// (`RekeyLimit`); 0 never starts one (the server still may). A small limit
/// is for tests.
pub void Transport.set_rekey_limit(int limit_bytes) {
    rekey_limit = limit_bytes;
}

/// Reads, authenticates and decrypts **one** packet. An application message
/// is returned as it is; `SSH_MSG_IGNORE`/`DEBUG`/`UNIMPLEMENTED` (transport
/// housekeeping RFC 4253 says any implementation must tolerate at any
/// time -- OpenSSH sends IGNORE as keystroke-timing chaff) come back as an
/// **empty** `bytes`; `SSH_MSG_DISCONNECT` is `Err(Error.Disconnected)`.
///
/// This is what an event loop calls after `wait(0)` said something was
/// there: unlike `recv` it never reads *another* packet after swallowing a
/// housekeeping one, which would block for ever on an idle connection.
pub Result<bytes, Error> Transport.recv_once() {
    if (queued.size() > 0) {
        bytes first = queued[0];
        queued.remove_at(0);
        return Result<bytes, Error>.Ok(first);
    }
    bytes payload = recv_ciphered_payload(connection, recv_cipher, recv_seq, budget_ms: read_budget_ms)?;
    recv_seq = recv_seq + 1;
    traffic = traffic + payload.size();
    if (payload.size() == 0) {
        return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.Truncated);
    }
    int message_type = payload[0];
    if (message_type == SSH_MESSAGE_DISCONNECT) {
        return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.Disconnected);
    }
    if (message_type == SSH_MESSAGE_IGNORE || message_type == SSH_MESSAGE_DEBUG || message_type == SSH_MESSAGE_UNIMPLEMENTED) {
        bytes none = [];
        return Result<bytes, Error>.Ok(none);
    }
    if (message_type == SSH_MESSAGE_KEXINIT) {
        // The server wants new keys (OpenSSH after 1 GB or an hour, or
        // whatever `RekeyLimit` says). Done here, invisibly to the layers
        // above: they see only the housekeeping-sized empty answer.
        rekey(payload)?;
        bytes none = [];
        return Result<bytes, Error>.Ok(none);
    }
    return Result<bytes, Error>.Ok(payload);
}

// One packet of a rekey, under the keys in force.
Result<void, Error> Transport.emit(bytes payload) {
    send_ciphered_payload(connection, payload, send_cipher, send_seq, rng)?;
    send_seq = send_seq + 1;
    return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
}

// The next packet of a rekey in progress: housekeeping (IGNORE, DEBUG) is
// skipped, DISCONNECT ends it, anything else is returned.
Result<bytes, Error> Transport.recv_for_rekey() {
    while (true) {
        bytes payload = recv_ciphered_payload(connection, recv_cipher, recv_seq, budget_ms: read_budget_ms)?;
        recv_seq = recv_seq + 1;
        if (payload.size() == 0) {
            return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.Truncated);
        }
        int message_type = payload[0];
        if (message_type == SSH_MESSAGE_DISCONNECT) {
            return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.Disconnected);
        }
        if (message_type != SSH_MESSAGE_IGNORE && message_type != SSH_MESSAGE_DEBUG) {
            return Result<bytes, Error>.Ok(payload);
        }
    }
}

// A key re-exchange answering the server's `KEXINIT` (`i_s`, already read):
// our own `KEXINIT`, the ECDH exchange, the signature checked against the
// SAME host key as the first exchange (a server whose key changes mid-session
// is refused, `HostKeyMismatch`), NEWKEYS both ways and fresh keys derived
// with the original session identifier. Strict kex, if it was agreed, still
// applies: sequence numbers restart at 0 after each NEWKEYS. Nothing but kex
// messages can arrive from the server until its NEWKEYS (RFC 4253 §7.1).
Result<void, Error> Transport.rekey(bytes i_s) {
    is_rekeying = true;
    bytes i_c = build_kexinit(rng, offer);
    emit(i_c)?;
    return finish_rekey(i_c, i_s);
}

// Our own re-exchange, at the traffic limit: KEXINIT first, then the
// server's packets until its KEXINIT arrives (application packets that were
// already on their way are kept for `recv_once`, in order), then the same
// exchange as when the server asks.
Result<void, Error> Transport.start_rekey() {
    is_rekeying = true;
    bytes i_c = build_kexinit(rng, offer);
    emit(i_c)?;
    while (true) {
        bytes payload = recv_for_rekey()?;
        if (payload[0] == SSH_MESSAGE_KEXINIT) {
            return finish_rekey(i_c, payload);
        }
        if (payload[0] != SSH_MESSAGE_UNIMPLEMENTED) {
            queued.push(payload);
        }
    }
}

Result<void, Error> Transport.finish_rekey(bytes i_c, bytes i_s) {
    KexInit server_kex = parse_kexinit(i_s)?;
    Suite fresh = negotiate(offer, server_kex)?;
    fresh.is_strict = negotiated.is_strict;
    EcdhState state = ecdh_begin(fresh, rng);
    emit(build_kex_ecdh_init(state.q_c))?;
    bytes reply_payload = recv_for_rekey()?;
    KexEcdhReply reply = parse_kex_ecdh_reply(reply_payload)?;
    if (reply.k_s != host_key_blob) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.HostKeyMismatch);
    }
    HostKey host_key = parse_host_key(reply.k_s)?;
    bytes k_mpint = ecdh_finish(fresh, state, reply.q_s)?;
    bytes digest = exchange_hash(versions.v_c, versions.v_s, i_c, i_s, reply.k_s, state.q_c, reply.q_s, k_mpint);
    verify_host_signature(host_key, fresh.host_key, digest, reply.signature)?;
    Exchange exchange = Exchange(host_key, k_mpint, digest, fresh, versions, offer);
    sshcipher.Cipher next_send = make_cipher(exchange, sid, fresh.cipher_client_to_server, fresh.mac_client_to_server, 'A', 'C', 'E');
    sshcipher.Cipher next_recv = make_cipher(exchange, sid, fresh.cipher_server_to_client, fresh.mac_server_to_client, 'B', 'D', 'F');
    emit([SSH_MESSAGE_NEWKEYS])?;
    send_cipher = next_send;
    if (fresh.is_strict) {
        send_seq = 0;
    }
    bytes their_newkeys = recv_for_rekey()?;
    if (their_newkeys.size() != 1 || their_newkeys[0] != SSH_MESSAGE_NEWKEYS) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.UnexpectedMessage);
    }
    recv_cipher = next_recv;
    if (fresh.is_strict) {
        recv_seq = 0;
    }
    negotiated = fresh;
    traffic = 0;
    is_rekeying = false;
    return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
}

/// Reads, authenticates and decrypts packets until one carries an
/// application-layer payload: housekeeping messages are absorbed (see
/// `recv_once`) so the layers above only ever see their own messages.
/// `SSH_MSG_DISCONNECT` becomes `Err(Error.Disconnected)`.
pub Result<bytes, Error> Transport.recv() {
    while (true) {
        bytes payload = recv_once()?;
        if (payload.size() > 0) {
            return Result<bytes, Error>.Ok(payload);
        }
    }
}

/// Bound every later read on this connection -- `recv`, and so every channel
/// read above it -- to `timeout_ms` milliseconds of waiting for the peer; 0
/// waits for ever, which is where a connection starts. A read that runs out
/// of time is `Err(Error.TimedOut)`, after which the transport must be
/// closed (see `Error.TimedOut`). It bounds one wait for bytes, not a whole
/// `recv`: a peer that sends a byte a second is never timed out by it.
///
/// A negative `timeout_ms` traps, as `net.Conn.set_read_timeout` does.
pub Result<void, Error> Transport.set_read_timeout(int timeout_ms) {
    read_budget_ms = timeout_ms;
    match (connection.set_read_timeout(timeout_ms)) {
        case Ok(bool is_set): {
            return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
        }
        case Err(_): {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.Io);
        }
    }
}

/// Whether a `recv` would find bytes without waiting: true when part of a
/// packet is already buffered or the socket is readable (which includes the
/// peer having closed); false when `timeout_ms` ran out first. A negative
/// timeout waits for ever. A true answer can still be followed by a `recv`
/// that waits for the rest of a packet, bounded by the read timeout.
pub Result<bool, Error> Transport.wait(int timeout_ms) {
    if (queued.size() > 0) {
        return Result<bool, Error>.Ok(true);
    }
    match (connection.wait(timeout_ms)) {
        case Ok(bool is_ready): {
            return Result<bool, Error>.Ok(is_ready);
        }
        case Err(_): {
            return Result<bool, Error>.Err(Error.Io);
        }
    }
}

/// The descriptor an event loop can `poll` for this connection's input. For a
/// tunnelled connection it is the outermost socket's: readable there means
/// "something may be waiting", and `wait(0)` is the exact answer.
pub int Transport.descriptor() {
    return connection.fileno();
}

/// Sends `SSH_MSG_DISCONNECT` (RFC 4253 §11.1, "by application") and closes.
/// Best effort: a send that fails still closes.
pub Result<bool, Error> Transport.disconnect() {
    bytes out = [];
    out.push(SSH_MESSAGE_DISCONNECT);
    push_u32(out, 11);
    push_string(out, "disconnected by user".to_bytes());
    push_string(out, "".to_bytes());
    Result<void, Error> ignored = send_ciphered_payload(connection, out, send_cipher, send_seq, rng);
    match (connection.close()) {
        case Ok(bool is_closed): {
            return Result<bool, Error>.Ok(is_closed);
        }
        case Err(_): {
            return Result<bool, Error>.Err(Error.Io);
        }
    }
}

/// Closes the underlying connection without a `SSH_MSG_DISCONNECT`
/// (`disconnect` sends one).
pub Result<bool, Error> Transport.close() {
    match (connection.close()) {
        case Ok(bool is_closed): {
            return Result<bool, Error>.Ok(is_closed);
        }
        case Err(_): {
            return Result<bool, Error>.Err(Error.Io);
        }
    }
}

/// How much traffic (both directions, payload bytes) this client lets pass
/// before it starts a rekey itself: 1 GiB, OpenSSH's default for these
/// ciphers. The server's own limit starts one earlier if it is lower.
const int DEFAULT_REKEY_LIMIT = 1073741824;

/// A host key as a server presented it. Public, so nothing here is secret.
pub type HostKey {
    /// The key type: `ssh-ed25519`, `ecdsa-sha2-nistp256` or `ssh-rsa`.
    pub str algorithm;
    /// The wire blob (`string type, ...`): what a `known_hosts` line's
    /// base64 column encodes and what OpenSSH hashes into a fingerprint.
    pub bytes blob;
    /// ed25519: the raw 32-octet key. ecdsa: the 65-octet point. rsa: empty
    /// (use `blob`).
    pub bytes key;
}

/// `SHA256:` and the unpadded base64 of the SHA-256 of the key blob, the
/// spelling `ssh-keygen -lf` and OpenSSH's host-key prompt use.
pub str HostKey.fingerprint() {
    str encoded = base64.encode(sha256.sum(blob));
    while (encoded.ends_with("=")) {
        encoded = encoded.substr(0, encoded.size() - 1);
    }
    return "SHA256:" + encoded;
}

/// The blob in base64: the third column of a `known_hosts` line, the second
/// of a `.pub` file.
pub str HostKey.blob_base64() {
    return base64.encode(blob);
}

// Whether `host_key` is exactly one of `allowed`. An empty list allows
// nothing -- a caller with no expectation gets a refusal, never trust on
// first use. Public keys are not secret, so plain `==` is fine.
bool is_key_listed(bytes host_key, List<bytes> allowed) {
    for (bytes allowed_key in allowed) {
        if (allowed_key == host_key) {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}

// Everything the first half of a handshake leaves behind: the key exchange
// is done and the server has proved, by signing the exchange hash, that it
// holds the private half of `host_key` -- but no key has been trusted and no
// `NEWKEYS` sent.
type Exchange {
    HostKey host_key;
    bytes k_mpint;
    bytes exchange_digest;
    Suite suite;
    VersionPair versions;
    Offer offer;
}

// The client half of an ECDH exchange: the curve, the ephemeral secret and
// the public value `Q_C` to send.
type EcdhState {
    ecdsa.Curve curve;
    bytes secret;
    bytes q_c;
}

EcdhState ecdh_begin(Suite suite, random.System rng) {
    ecdsa.Curve curve = ecdsa.p256();
    bytes secret = [];
    bytes q_c = [];
    if (suite.kex == KEX_ECDH_P256) {
        secret = rng.octets(32);
        while (!ecdsa.is_secret_scalar(curve, secret)) {
            secret = rng.octets(32);
        }
        q_c = ecdsa.ephemeral_public(curve, secret);
    } else {
        secret = rng.octets(32);
        q_c = x25519.base_point(secret);
    }
    return EcdhState(curve, secret, q_c);
}

// The shared secret `K` as an mpint, from the server's public value `q_s`,
// or `BadKeyExchange` for a value that is not valid (off the curve, the
// wrong size, or of small order).
Result<bytes, Error> ecdh_finish(Suite suite, EcdhState state, bytes q_s) {
    bytes shared = [];
    if (suite.kex == KEX_ECDH_P256) {
        match (ecdsa.parse_public_key(state.curve, q_s)) {
            case Some(ecdsa.PublicKey peer): {
                shared = ecdsa.shared_secret(state.curve, peer, state.secret);
            }
            case None: {
                return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.BadKeyExchange);
            }
        }
    } else {
        if (q_s.size() != 32) {
            return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.BadKeyExchange);
        }
        // RFC 8731 §3.1's text says this gets reversed (little-endian, RFC
        // 7748's own output, into big-endian before mpint-wrapping) -- but a
        // real `sshd` rejects the host key signature when that reversal is
        // applied, and accepts it when `x25519`'s raw output is mpint-encoded
        // directly, confirmed with `tests/ssh/test_transport.sh`'s live
        // fixture. This is the actual, shipped OpenSSH behavior.
        shared = x25519.x25519(state.secret, q_s);
        // RFC 7748 §6.1 / RFC 8731: a peer value of small order makes the
        // output all zero; OpenSSH refuses it and so do we.
        if (is_all_zero_octets(shared)) {
            return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.BadKeyExchange);
        }
    }
    return Result<bytes, Error>.Ok(encode_mpint(shared));
}

// Version exchange, KEXINIT both ways, the ECDH exchange and the check of the
// host key's signature -- the part of a handshake that is the same whether or
// not the key is one the caller trusts.
//
// Strict kex (OpenSSH 9.6, the Terrapin fix): if both sides advertise it, the
// handshake must be exactly KEXINIT, the exchange and NEWKEYS with nothing
// in between (this function reads only those messages and treats any other
// -- including IGNORE and DEBUG -- as `UnexpectedMessage`), and the sequence
// numbers restart at 0 after NEWKEYS (`finish_handshake`). Without the peer's
// marker the old behaviour is kept so older servers still work.
Result<Exchange, Error> exchange_keys(Wire connection, random.System rng, Offer offer) {
    VersionPair version_pair = exchange_version(connection)?;
    bytes i_c = build_kexinit(rng, offer);
    send_plain_payload(connection, i_c, rng)?;
    bytes i_s = recv_plain_payload(connection)?;
    KexInit server_kex = parse_kexinit(i_s)?;
    Suite suite = negotiate(offer, server_kex)?;
    EcdhState state = ecdh_begin(suite, rng);
    bytes q_c = state.q_c;
    send_plain_payload(connection, build_kex_ecdh_init(q_c), rng)?;
    bytes reply_payload = recv_plain_payload(connection)?;
    KexEcdhReply reply = parse_kex_ecdh_reply(reply_payload)?;
    HostKey host_key = parse_host_key(reply.k_s)?;
    bytes k_mpint = ecdh_finish(suite, state, reply.q_s)?;
    bytes exchange_digest = exchange_hash(version_pair.v_c, version_pair.v_s, i_c, i_s, reply.k_s, q_c, reply.q_s, k_mpint);
    verify_host_signature(host_key, suite.host_key, exchange_digest, reply.signature)?;
    return Result<Exchange, Error>.Ok(Exchange(host_key, k_mpint, exchange_digest, suite, version_pair, offer));
}

// Whether every octet is zero. The value is a key-exchange output that is
// about to be rejected or used; a plain scan is fine, but the whole buffer is
// always read.
bool is_all_zero_octets(bytes value) {
    int accumulated = 0;
    for (int index in 0 .. value.size()) {
        accumulated = accumulated | value[index];
    }
    return accumulated == 0;
}

// Key sizes of the ciphers this client has: (key octets, iv octets).
int cipher_key_size(str name) {
    if (name == CIPHER_ALGO) {
        return 64;
    }
    if (name == CIPHER_GCM256 || name == CIPHER_CTR256) {
        return 32;
    }
    return 16;
}

int cipher_iv_size(str name) {
    if (name == CIPHER_ALGO) {
        return 0;
    }
    if (name == CIPHER_GCM256 || name == CIPHER_GCM128) {
        return 12;
    }
    return 16;
}

// One direction's cipher from the exchange: letters as RFC 4253 §7.2
// (IV A/B, key C/D, integrity E/F for client-to-server/server-to-client).
sshcipher.Cipher make_cipher(Exchange exchange, bytes session_id, str name, str mac, int iv_letter, int key_letter, int mac_letter) {
    bytes key = derive(exchange.k_mpint, exchange.exchange_digest, key_letter, session_id, cipher_key_size(name));
    if (name == CIPHER_ALGO) {
        return sshcipher.new_chacha(key);
    }
    bytes initial_vector = derive(exchange.k_mpint, exchange.exchange_digest, iv_letter, session_id, cipher_iv_size(name));
    if (name == CIPHER_GCM256 || name == CIPHER_GCM128) {
        return sshcipher.new_aes_gcm(key, initial_vector);
    }
    bytes mac_key = derive(exchange.k_mpint, exchange.exchange_digest, mac_letter, session_id, 32);
    return sshcipher.new_aes_ctr(key, initial_vector, mac_key, mac == MAC_ETM);
}

// The rest of the handshake, given an exchange whose host key the caller has
// accepted: key derivation, then NEWKEYS both ways.
Result<Transport, Error> finish_handshake(Wire connection, Exchange exchange, random.System rng) {
    int send_seq = 2;
    int recv_seq = 2;
    sshcipher.Cipher send_cipher = make_cipher(exchange, exchange.exchange_digest, exchange.suite.cipher_client_to_server, exchange.suite.mac_client_to_server, 'A', 'C', 'E');
    sshcipher.Cipher recv_cipher = make_cipher(exchange, exchange.exchange_digest, exchange.suite.cipher_server_to_client, exchange.suite.mac_server_to_client, 'B', 'D', 'F');
    send_plain_payload(connection, [SSH_MESSAGE_NEWKEYS], rng)?;
    send_seq = send_seq + 1;
    bytes their_newkeys = recv_plain_payload(connection)?;
    recv_seq = recv_seq + 1;
    if (their_newkeys.size() != 1 || their_newkeys[0] != SSH_MESSAGE_NEWKEYS) {
        return Result<Transport, Error>.Err(Error.UnexpectedMessage);
    }
    if (exchange.suite.is_strict) {
        send_seq = 0;
        recv_seq = 0;
    }
    return Result<Transport, Error>.Ok(Transport(connection, send_seq, recv_seq, send_cipher, recv_cipher, exchange.exchange_digest, rng, exchange.suite, exchange.versions, exchange.offer, exchange.host_key.blob, 0, DEFAULT_REKEY_LIMIT, [], false, 0));
}

/// What decides, mid-handshake, whether the server's host key is trusted:
/// `is_acceptable` is called once, after the key's signature over the
/// exchange hash has verified (so `key` really belongs to whoever answered)
/// and before any key is derived or any credential offered. `false` ends the
/// handshake with `Error.HostKeyMismatch`. It may ask a person; it must not
/// record anything it was not told to.
pub interface HostKeyCheck {
    bool is_acceptable(HostKey key);
}

/// The handshake over a connection the caller already made (so it can tell a
/// refused connection from an unreachable one), with the host-key decision
/// handed to `check` instead of a fixed list and the algorithms `offer`.
/// One connection, one key exchange: nothing is asked of the server twice.
pub Result<Transport, Error> connect_with_offer(Wire connection, HostKeyCheck check, Offer offer, int timeout_ms = 0) {
    if (timeout_ms > 0) {
        match (connection.set_read_timeout(timeout_ms)) {
            case Ok(bool is_set): {
            }
            case Err(_): {
                return Result<Transport, Error>.Err(Error.Io);
            }
        }
    }
    random.System rng = random.system();
    Exchange exchange = exchange_keys(connection, rng, offer)?;
    if (!check.is_acceptable(exchange.host_key)) {
        return Result<Transport, Error>.Err(Error.HostKeyMismatch);
    }
    Transport transport = finish_handshake(connection, exchange, rng)?;
    transport.read_budget_ms = timeout_ms;
    return Result<Transport, Error>.Ok(transport);
}

/// `connect_with_offer` with `default_offer()`.
pub Result<Transport, Error> connect_over(Wire connection, HostKeyCheck check, int timeout_ms = 0) {
    return connect_with_offer(connection, check, default_offer(), timeout_ms: timeout_ms);
}

// A fixed list of acceptable raw ed25519 keys, as `connect_any` takes.
type ListedKeys {
    List<bytes> keys;
}

bool ListedKeys.is_acceptable(HostKey key) {
    return key.algorithm == HOST_KEY_ALGO && is_key_listed(key.key, keys);
}

/// Connects to `host`:`port`, runs the whole handshake, and refuses unless
/// the server's host key is exactly `expected_host_key` (32 raw
/// `ssh-ed25519` public-key octets) -- `docs/ssh-decision.md` §1's "v0 takes
/// an expected host key as a parameter and refuses to proceed if it doesn't
/// match."
pub Result<Transport, Error> connect(str host, int port, bytes expected_host_key, int timeout_ms = 0) {
    return connect_any(host, port, [expected_host_key], timeout_ms: timeout_ms);
}

/// As `connect`, but any one of `host_keys` is acceptable -- a host that
/// has rotated keys, or a `known_hosts` file listing several. An empty list
/// refuses every server (`Error.HostKeyMismatch`): there is no trust on
/// first use. Only `ssh-ed25519` host keys are asked for.
pub Result<Transport, Error> connect_any(str host, int port, List<bytes> host_keys, int timeout_ms = 0) {
    match (net.connect(host, port)) {
        case Ok(net.Conn connection): {
            Offer offer = offer_preferring([HOST_KEY_ALGO]);
            offer.host_keys = [HOST_KEY_ALGO];
            return connect_with_offer(connection, ListedKeys(host_keys), offer, timeout_ms: timeout_ms);
        }
        case Err(_): {
            return Result<Transport, Error>.Err(Error.Io);
        }
    }
}

// Accepts any key: only for `presented_host_key`, which trusts nothing.
type AnyKey {
    int unused;
}

bool AnyKey.is_acceptable(HostKey key) {
    return true;
}

/// The host key the server at `host`:`port` presents, for a caller that
/// wants to ask a person whether to trust it (`key.fingerprint()`, then
/// `sshhosts.add`). Runs the key exchange as `connect_any` does, including
/// the check of the server's signature -- so the key really belongs to
/// whoever answered -- and then hangs up: nothing is trusted, no
/// credentials are offered, and no session exists afterwards.
///
/// This is a second connection. That is safe: it decides only what to
/// *show*; the key the person approves is then enforced by an ordinary
/// `connect_any`, which refuses any other. (`connect_over` with a
/// `HostKeyCheck` decides on the one connection and is what `m31ssh` uses.)
pub Result<HostKey, Error> presented_host_key(str host, int port) {
    match (net.connect(host, port)) {
        case Ok(net.Conn connection): {
            Result<Exchange, Error> got = exchange_keys(connection, random.system(), default_offer());
            match (connection.close()) {
                case Ok(bool done): {
                }
                case Err(io.Error close_error): {
                }
            }
            Exchange exchange = got?;
            return Result<HostKey, Error>.Ok(exchange.host_key);
        }
        case Err(net.Error error): {
            return Result<HostKey, Error>.Err(Error.Io);
        }
    }
}
